{"built":"2026-08-25","axes":{"x":{"from":"cold","to":"warm","read":"temperature, as his weather notes describe it"},"y":{"from":"one thing","to":"everything","read":"the scale the work asks you to look at"}},"items":[{"id":"caspar-david-friedrich-the-monk-by-the-sea","type":"painting","who":"Caspar David Friedrich","title":"The Monk by the Sea","line":"The Monk by the Sea","year":"1808–1810","where":"","weather":"vastness","src":"/images/today/friedrich.webp","url":"","note":"Kleist wrote that looking at it felt as though his eyelids had been cut away. Friedrich painted out the ships he had first put on the horizon, leaving nothing between the figure and the sky.","added":"2026-08-15","x":-0.2503,"y":0.9702,"unplaced":false},{"id":"claude-monet-water-lilies","type":"painting","who":"Claude Monet","title":"Water Lilies","line":"Water Lilies","year":"c. 1915","where":"","weather":"invincible summer","src":"/images/today/monet.webp","url":"","note":"From the pond he dug himself at Giverny and then spent thirty years painting. These late ones were made as his cataracts thickened, which is part of why the lilies stop having edges.","added":"2026-08-15","x":0.8326,"y":0.3538,"unplaced":false},{"id":"hilma-af-klint-the-ten-largest-no-7-adulthood","type":"painting","who":"Hilma af Klint","title":"The Ten Largest, No. 7, Adulthood","line":"The Ten Largest, No. 7, Adulthood","year":"1907","where":"","weather":"weight and grace","src":"/images/today/afklint.webp","url":"","note":"Painted in 1907, years before abstraction was credited to anyone else. She left instructions that the work not be shown until twenty years after her death, and it was not shown properly for forty.","added":"2026-08-15","x":0.2742,"y":0.2079,"unplaced":false},{"id":"his-dock","type":"painting","who":"Dmytro Klochko","title":"dock","line":"dock","year":"","where":"","weather":"","src":"/images/scenes/dock.webp","url":"","note":"the boy fishing. stands behind childhood.","added":"2026-08-18","x":0,"y":-