rue},{"id":"his-study","type":"painting","who":"Dmytro Klochko","title":"study","line":"study","year":"","where":"","weather":"","src":"/images/scenes/study.webp","url":"","note":"one of his own, taken down to night, for learning.","added":"2026-08-18","x":1.0542,"y":0.1108,"unplaced":true},{"id":"his-swim","type":"painting","who":"Dmytro Klochko","title":"swim","line":"swim","year":"","where":"","weather":"","src":"/images/scenes/swim.webp","url":"","note":"the swimmer under the orange sun. the opening screen.","added":"2026-08-18","x":1.0081,"y":0.3276,"unplaced":true},{"id":"j-m-w-turner-norham-castle-sunrise","type":"painting","who":"J. M. W. Turner","title":"Norham Castle, Sunrise","line":"Norham Castle, Sunrise","year":"c. 1845","where":"","weather":"dissolution","src":"/images/today/turner.webp","url":"","note":"Never exhibited. It was found in his studio after he died, one of a group he seems to have painted for himself, and the castle has almost finished dissolving into the light.","added":"2026-08-15","x":-0.445,"y":0.5565,"unplaced":false},{"id":"j-m-w-turner-staffa-fingals-cave","type":"painting","who":"J. M. W. Turner","title":"Staffa, Fingal's Cave","line":"Staffa, Fingal's Cave","year":"1831–32","where":"","weather":"nerve","src":"/images/today/turner-staffa.webp","url":"","note":"Turner took the steamer to Staffa in bad weather, and by his own account the\ncrossing was rough enough that most of the passengers gave up. He stayed on\ndeck. It was the first of his paintings to be sold in America.","added":"2026-08-15","x":0.9483,"y":-0.0973,"unplaced":false},{"id":"james-mcneill-whistler-nocturne-blue-and-gold-old-battersea-","type":"painting","who":"James McNeill Whistler","title":"Nocturne: Blue and Gold — Old Battersea Bridge","line":"Nocturne: Blue and Gold — Old Battersea Bridge","year":"c. 1872","where":"","weather":"the dark and the lamp","src":"/images/today/whistler.webp","url":"","note":"Ruskin said Whistler was flinging a pot of paint in the public's face. Whistler sued him for libel, won, and was awarded one farthing in damages, which bankrupted him.","added":"2026-08-15","x":0.1899,"y":-0.6,"unplaced":false},{"id":"jean-baptiste-sim-on-chardin-a-bowl-of-plums","type":"painting","who":"Jean-Baptiste-Siméon Chardin","title":"A Bowl of Plums","line":"A Bowl of Plums","year":"c. 1728","where":"","weather":"the plain thing","src":"/images/today/chardin.webp","url":"","note":"Chardin painted more or less the same handful of objects for forty years. Diderot said of him that this one understands the harmony of colours and reflections, and the rest of us paint things.","added":"2026-08-15","x":-0.0525,"y":-0.8961,"unplaced":false},{"id":"vilhelm-hammersh-i-dust-motes-dancing-in-the-sunbeams","type":"painting","who":"Vilhelm Hammershøi","title":"Dust Motes Dancing in the Sunbeams","line":"Dust Motes Dancing in the Sunbeams","year":"1900","where":"","weather":"cold clarity","src":"/images/today/hammershoi.webp","url":"","note":"Painted in his own flat at Strandgade 30, which he painted again and again for a decade. No furniture to speak of, no story, nobody home: an empty room and the light crossing it.","added":"2026-08-15","x":-0.8501,"y":-0.3801,"unplaced":false},{"id":"attributed-to-sappho-the-moon-has-set-and-the-pleiades-it-i","type":"poem","who":"attributed to Sappho","title":"","line":"The moon has set,","year":"","where":"fragment 168B","weather":"the dark and the lamp","src":"","url":"","note":"Given to Sappho since the Renaissance, though most scholars now think it is a folk song by someone whose name is gone. Four lines outlived their author entirely.","added":"2026-08-15","x":0.0526,"y":-0.5531,"unplaced":false},{"id":"bash-even-the-horse-holds-my-eye-snow-on-th","type":"poem","who":"Bashō","title":"","line":"even the horse","year":"","where":"1684","weather":"cold clarity","src":"","url":"","note":"Written on the road in his first travel journal, the one he set out on expecting to die of exposure. He noticed a horse instead.","added":"2026-08-15","x":-1.0145,"y":-0.3795,"unplaced":false},{"id":"bash-the-old-pond-a-frog-jumps-in-the-soun","type":"poem","who":"Bashō","title":"","line":"the old pond —","year":"","where":"1686","weather":"the plain thing","src":"","url":"","note":"The most famous poem in Japanese. His students argued for years about the first line; he kept the plainest one.","added":"2026-08-15","x":-0.197,"y":-0.8638,"unplaced":false},{"id":"cavafy-honour-to-those-who-in-their-lives","type":"poem","who":"C. P. Cavafy","title":"","line":"Honour to those who in their lives","year":"","where":"Thermopylae, printed in Alexandrian Art, 1927","weather":"nerve","src":"","url":"","note":"Thermopylae is the pass the Spartans held knowing they would lose it, and Ephialtes is the man who showed the Persians the way around. Cavafy's point is that the traitor arriving does not cancel the holding.","added":"2026-08-15","x":0.7841,"y":-0.1335,"unplaced":false},{"id":"emily-dickinson-to-make-a-prairie-it-takes-a-clover-and","type":"poem","who":"Emily Dickinson","title":"","line":"To make a prairie it takes a clover and one bee,","year":"","where":"c. 1879","weather":"invincible summer","src":"","url":"","note":"Found among her papers after her death, unpublished in her lifetime, like almost everything she wrote.","added":"2026-08-15","x":0.6846,"y":0.3983,"unplaced":false},{"id":"fernando-pessoa-the-poet-is-a-faker-he-fakes-it-so-comp","type":"poem","who":"Fernando Pessoa","title":"","line":"The poet is a faker.","year":"","where":"Autopsicografia, 1932","weather":"dissolution","src":"","url":"","note":"Pessoa published under seventy-two invented names, each with a biography and a handwriting. This one he signed himself.","added":"2026-08-15","x":-0.58,"y":0.5723,"unplaced":false},{"id":"rainer-maria-rilke-whoever-has-no-house-now-will-not-build","type":"poem","who":"Rainer Maria Rilke","title":"","line":"Whoever has no house now will not build one.","year":"","where":"Herbsttag, 1902","weather":"weight and grace","src":"","url":"","note":"Written in Paris in a single September day, at twenty-six, while he was working as Rodin's secretary and learning to look at things.","added":"2026-08-15","x":0.1287,"y":0.2629,"unplaced":false},{"id":"wang-wei-empty-mountain-no-one-in-sight-only-t","type":"poem","who":"Wang Wei","title":"","line":"There seems to be no one on the empty mountain…","year":"","where":"Deer Park, 8th century","weather":"vastness","src":"","url":"","note":"Twenty characters that have been translated into English more than a hundred times, no two alike. Nobody agrees where the light is coming from.","added":"2026-08-15","x":-0.4163,"y":0.9608,"unplaced":false},{"id":"bill-evans-peace-piece","type":"song","who":"Bill Evans","title":"Peace Piece","line":"Peace Piece","year":"1958","where":"","weather":"the plain thing","src":"","url":"","note":"One left-hand figure, held for seven minutes, improvised in a single take at the end of a session.","added":"2026-08-15","x":-0.0719,"y":-1.0403,"unplaced":false},{"id":"chet-baker-almost-blue","type":"song","who":"Chet Baker","title":"Almost Blue","line":"Almost Blue","year":"1987","where":"","weather":"the dark and the lamp","src":"","url":"","note":"Elvis Costello wrote it about a Chet Baker song. Baker then recorded it, near the end, and it sounds like it was always his.","added":"2026-08-15","x":0.1973,"y":-0.7324,"unplaced":false},{"id":"eno-an-ending","type":"song","who":"Brian Eno","title":"An Ending (Ascent)","line":"An Ending (Ascent)","year":"1983","where":"","weather":"dissolution","src":"","url":"","note":"From a soundtrack to a documentary about the Apollo missions, made for the parts where nothing happens.","added":"2026-08-15","x":-0.4303,"y":0.405,"unplaced":false},{"id":"nina-simone-feeling-good","type":"song","who":"Nina Simone","title":"Feeling Good","line":"Feeling Good","year":"1965","where":"","weather":"invincible summer","src":"","url":"","note":"Written for a musical nobody remembers. She opens it alone, with no band at all, for forty seconds.","added":"2026-08-15","x":0.8672,"y":0.2429,"unplaced":false},{"id":"part-spiegel-im-spiegel","type":"song","who":"Arvo Pärt","title":"Spiegel im Spiegel","line":"Spiegel im Spiegel","year":"1978","where":"","weather":"weight and grace","src":"","url":"","note":"Mirror in the mirror: a piano triad and a violin line that never hurries, written just before he left Estonia.","added":"2026-08-15","x":0.2685,"y":0.0846,"unplaced":false},{"id":"satie-gymnopedie","type":"song","who":"Erik Satie","title":"Gymnopédie No. 1","line":"Gymnopédie No. 1","year":"1888","where":"","weather":"cold clarity","src":"","url":"","note":"Three pieces written when he was twenty-two and playing piano in a Montmartre cabaret. Nobody could place them; he called himself a phonometrician rather than a musician.","added":"2026-08-15","x":-0.8103,"y":-0.4954,"unplaced":false},{"id":"seatbelts-tank","type":"song","who":"Seatbelts","title":"Tank!","line":"Tank!","year":"1998","where":"","weather":"nerve","src":"","url":"","note":"Yoko Kanno wrote a big-band opening for a cartoon about broke bounty hunters, and it swings harder than most things written for anything.","added":"2026-08-15","x":0.9717,"y":-0.2261,"unplaced":false},{"id":"vangelis-blade-runner-blues","type":"song","who":"Vangelis","title":"Blade Runner Blues","line":"Blade Runner Blues","year":"1982","where":"","weather":"vastness","src":"","url":"","note":"Recorded live to picture, improvised while the film ran, which is why it breathes with the city rather than under it.","added":"2026-08-15","x":-0.1837,"y":0.8789,"unplaced":false},{"id":"albert-camus-in-the-midst-of-winter-i-finally-learne","type":"quote","who":"Albert Camus","title":"","line":"Au milieu de l’hiver, j’apprenais enfin qu’il y avait en moi un été invincible.","year":"","where":"Retour à Tipasa, 1952","weather":"invincible summer","src":"","url":"","note":"","added":"20